# Design Contract

Use the pinned DenchCo visual adapter as the baseline, then record only deliberate target-specific token and interaction decisions here. Navigation and synthesis must follow the target's audience and governing question.

Record how the confirmed accent generated its light, dark, visited, transparent, and contrast tokens. Verify at least 4.5:1 contrast for normal accent-coloured text and 3:1 for non-text interactive boundaries against their rendered backgrounds.

For the dual-appearance reference adapter, configure Zensical's native `default` and `slate` palettes, record the consumer's initial palette, and keep the sun/moon action immediately left of Wide. Preserve native keyboard operation and persistence, including mobile where Wide is hidden. Record a complete named colour family for each appearance; adapt scheme-scoped renderer/control aliases, Mermaid variables and the same-origin graph theme bridge together. Verify both appearances, focus and selected-state contrast, navigation/reload persistence, search, diagrams, 2D/3D graph views and contained mobile geometry. Do not introduce competing preference storage or infer a system preference without configuring that behaviour in Zensical.

For Zensical 0.0.59, retain the reference adapter that reflects the resolved body scheme into the native radio's checked state. Check keyboard entry and visible focus before interacting with the restored control, on first load and after reload or navigation; do not conceal restoration faults by cycling both appearances first.

Every displayed governing-question callout must expose the `governing-question` marker and use the active accent colour for both its inline-start rail and question text at every supported viewport. When the Wiki declares one canonical question, every verbatim repetition in its bounded reader set uses that governed component. Paraphrases and unrelated quotations remain neutral.

When primary navigation exposes a page's `draft` status, adapt the pinned Pen Circle marker and “Draft — research in progress” tooltip with the complete renderer asset closure. Align the marker's trailing centre with the open/close chevron column in the supported desktop navigation. Other lifecycle states keep distinct semantics and artwork.

When the Human Wiki displays registered source identities, render each one as an independently focusable internal link to its exact source-register row while preserving the target's declared citation grammar. Keep an intelligible source identity as the link's accessible name, retain native focus indication, and verify the representative journey at desktop and mobile widths.

When the Human Wiki uses Mermaid, begin with the pinned renderer's ordinary node treatment. Ordinary entry and architecture diagrams use no diagram-level `init`, `class`, `classDef`, `style`, or `linkStyle` directives merely to restyle their nodes. Preserve accessible titles and descriptions, and express authority or derivation through labels, order, and adjacent prose. The pinned semantic roles (`kb-source`, `kb-evidence`, `kb-snapshot`, `kb-normative`, `kb-canonical`, `kb-product`, `kb-derived`, `kb-consumer`, and `kb-verification`) remain available only when a material distinction genuinely requires visual reinforcement; they are not the default and may never carry meaning alone. Entry and architecture diagrams use a top-to-bottom reading direction, Mermaid's native `basis` connectors, consistent rectangular node geometry, ordinary arrows, short labels, and no edge labels. Other reference diagrams use no more than five nodes and five visible relationships. The single bounded three-product fan-out may use up to seven nodes and eight visible relationships and counts as one coordinated branch group. When Human, Agent, and Graph surfaces are all declared and shown, use three separately labelled sibling boxes reached directly from canonical knowledge; where verification follows, each path rejoins it. The separation represents three views over one shared corpus, not separate knowledge authorities. Inherit renderer-default node, rank, and padding values unless a bounded, recorded override is necessary for content-rail fit or legibility. Move finer detail into nearby prose or a separate diagram. Connectors must not intersect text and nodes must not overlap; verify the rendered result at desktop, tablet, and mobile widths. Centre a diagram surface whenever its intrinsic width fits the content rail. After labels are shortened and renderer defaults retained, the bounded three-product branch may exceed the 390px reference content rail by no more than 60px; safe centring falls back to start alignment for that oversized surface, its pane opens at the horizontal centre, and no page overflow is created.
